## Is rheumatoid arthritis a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Because RA is long-term and its effects without treatment are usually substantial, most people with RA are covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- flexible hours, or a later start if mornings are stiff
- working from home during flares
- time off for appointments and infusions
- adapted equipment, such as a different keyboard, mouse or chair
- avoiding heavy lifting or long periods of standing

**Access to Work** can help pay for equipment, taxis to work, or support. NRAS has free guides on working with RA.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how RA affects you on most days. It can affect activities such as:

- **preparing food**, if your hands are painful or weak
- **washing and dressing**, including buttons, zips and shoes
- **managing treatments**, if you need help with injections
- **moving around**, if your feet, knees or hips are affected

Think about your worst days and how often they happen. Fatigue counts if it stops you doing things reliably.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
